# 1. Difference Between `class` and `struct`

A **class** is a **reference type**, while a **struct** is a **value type**.

| Feature                                      | Class                           | Struct                            |
| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| **Type**                                     | Reference type                  | Value type                        |
| **Represents**                               | Usually an entity/object        | Usually a small value             |
| **Assignment**                               | Copies the reference            | Copies the value                  |
| **Can have methods**                         | ✅                               | ✅                                 |
| **Can have properties**                      | ✅                               | ✅                                 |
| **Can implement interfaces**                 | ✅                               | ✅                                 |
| **Inheritance from another class**           | ✅                               | ❌                                 |
| **Can inherit another struct**               | ❌                               | ❌                                 |
| **Can be inherited by another class/struct** | ✅                               | ❌                                 |
| **Default value**                            | `null`                          | Default/zero value                |
| **Typical use**                              | `Customer`, `Order`, `Employee` | `Point`, `DateTime`, small values |

### Example

```csharp
class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

struct Point
{
    public int X;
    public int Y;
}
```

### Class assignment

```csharp
Person p1 = new Person();
p1.Name = "John";

Person p2 = p1;

p2.Name = "Alex";

Console.WriteLine(p1.Name); // Alex
```

Both `p1` and `p2` refer to the **same object**.

### Struct assignment

```csharp
Point p1 = new Point();
p1.X = 10;

Point p2 = p1;

p2.X = 20;

Console.WriteLine(p1.X); // 10
Console.WriteLine(p2.X); // 20
```

`p1` and `p2` contain **separate copies of the value**.

---

## When to Use a Struct?

Use a struct when:

* The data is relatively **small**
* You want **value-type behavior**
* The object represents a **value rather than an entity**
* You don't need class inheritance
* The value is logically independent when copied

### Examples

```text
Point
DateTime
TimeSpan
Guid
```

> 💡 **Easy way to remember:**
> **Class → Object/Entity**
> **Struct → Small Value**

