# 3. Abstraction

### Definition

> **Abstraction** means hiding unnecessary implementation details and exposing only the essential functionality.

In simple words:

```text
What to do
   ↓
Expose
   ↓
How it is done
   ↓
Hide
```

### Example

```csharp
public abstract class Payment
{
    public abstract void ProcessPayment(decimal amount);

    public void ShowReceipt()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Payment Successful");
    }
}
```

```csharp
public class CreditCardPayment : Payment
{
    public override void ProcessPayment(decimal amount)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(
            $"Processing Credit Card payment of {amount}"
        );
    }
}
```

### Usage

```csharp
Payment payment = new CreditCardPayment();

payment.ProcessPayment(1000);
payment.ShowReceipt();
```

### Output

```text
Processing Credit Card payment of 1000
Payment Successful
```

### Key Points

* Abstract class cannot normally be instantiated directly
* Abstract methods don't contain implementation in the base class
* Derived classes provide the implementation
* Hides implementation details
* Focuses on **what** an object does rather than **how**

### Real-life Example

🏧 **ATM**

You know:

```text
Insert Card
Enter PIN
Withdraw Money
```

But you don't need to know the internal banking system implementation.

> 💡 **Encapsulation vs Abstraction**
>
> **Encapsulation → How to protect data?**
> **Abstraction → How to hide implementation complexity?**

