# 6. Interface vs Abstract Class

Both **interfaces** and **abstract classes** are used to define contracts/abstractions, but they serve different purposes.

| Feature                   | Abstract Class                                    | Interface                                                                                                                 |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**               | Base class with shared abstraction/implementation | Defines a contract/capability                                                                                             |
| **Method implementation** | Can have implemented methods                      | Can have implementations using modern C# features                                                                         |
| **Abstract methods**      | ✅                                                 | ✅                                                                                                                         |
| **Fields**                | ✅                                                 | ❌ Instance fields                                                                                                         |
| **Constants**             | ✅                                                 | ✅                                                                                                                         |
| **Constructor**           | ✅                                                 | ❌                                                                                                                         |
| **Instance state**        | ✅                                                 | ❌                                                                                                                         |
| **Access modifiers**      | `private`, `protected`, `public`, etc.            | Interface members are generally public contract members; explicit implementations can be private to the implementing type |
| **Multiple inheritance**  | ❌ A class can inherit only one class              | ✅ A class can implement multiple interfaces                                                                               |
| **Use when**              | Classes are closely related                       | Different classes share a capability/contract                                                                             |

---

## Abstract Class Example

```csharp
public abstract class Vehicle
{
    public string Brand { get; set; }

    public void Start()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Vehicle started");
    }

    public abstract void Move();
}
```

```csharp
public class Car : Vehicle
{
    public override void Move()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Car is moving");
    }
}
```

Here:

```text
Vehicle
   ↑
   |
  Car
```

`Car` **IS-A** `Vehicle`.

Use an abstract class when the derived classes are **closely related** and should share common state or implementation.

